==========stderr==========
These steps could each follow an edge that another of them creates, so mk has no order to take them in: `c/!{@000000000002}` and the node where `a` and `c` meet. If a step means the same edge as a step into a node where steps meet, leave it out and write what follows it after that node, as in (a & c)/@/b for a/b & (a & c)/@; otherwise write the node a step should go to (/@N after it), or create them with separate commands. Nothing was changed. (CircularSuppliers)
